## Migration Analysis

Your code has three patterns that break on .NET 8:

| API | Problem on .NET 8 |
|-----|-------------------|
| `Thread.Interrupt()` | Obsolete (`SYSLIB0046` in .NET 9), unreliable wake mechanism |
| `Thread.Abort()` | Throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` |
| `ManualResetEvent.WaitOne()` | Not cancellation-aware — can't be woken cooperatively |

## Migrated Code (.NET 8)

```csharp
public class EventProcessor
{
    private readonly CancellationTokenSource _cts = new();
    private readonly BlockingCollection<string> _queue = new();
    private Thread? _thread;

    public void Start()
    {
        _thread = new Thread(RunLoop);
        _thread.Start();
    }

    private void RunLoop()
    {
        try
        {
            foreach (var item in _queue.GetConsumingEnumerable(_cts.Token))
            {
                Handle(item);
            }
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException)
        {
            // Normal shutdown — token was cancelled
        }
    }

    public void Enqueue(string item)
    {
        _queue.Add(item);
    }

    public void Stop()
    {
        _cts.Cancel();          // signals the loop to exit
        _queue.CompleteAdding(); // unblocks GetConsumingEnumerable
        _thread?.Join(5000);    // wait for graceful exit
        _cts.Dispose();
        _queue.Dispose();
    }

    private void Handle(string item) { /* ... */ }
}
```

## Why This Works

- **`BlockingCollection<T>`** replaces the manual `Queue` + `ManualResetEvent` + `lock` — it's thread-safe and blocks the consumer until items arrive.
- **`CancellationToken`** replaces both `Thread.Interrupt` and `Thread.Abort` — `GetConsumingEnumerable` checks the token internally and throws `OperationCanceledException` to exit cleanly.
- **No force-kill needed** — cooperative cancellation means the thread exits voluntarily.

## Key Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Mitigation |
|---------|------------|
| `Handle()` runs a long operation that ignores cancellation | Pass `_cts.Token` into `Handle` and check `token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested()` at checkpoints |
| Calling `Enqueue` after `Stop` | `_queue.Add` throws `InvalidOperationException` after `CompleteAdding()` — guard with `_queue.IsAddingCompleted` or catch it |
| Not disposing `CancellationTokenSource` | Leaks timer/callback resources — always dispose in `Stop` or via `IDisposable` |
| Expecting instant stop during `Handle` | Cancellation only triggers between items unless `Handle` cooperates — design accordingly |

## If You Need the Manual WaitHandle Approach

If `BlockingCollection` doesn't fit your design, here's the equivalent using `WaitHandle.WaitAny`:

```csharp
private void RunLoop()
{
    var handles = new[] { _newEvent, _cts.Token.WaitHandle };
    while (!_cts.Token.IsCancellationRequested)
    {
        int index = WaitHandle.WaitAny(handles); // wakes on either signal
        if (index == 1) break; // cancellation handle fired

        _newEvent.Reset();
        string item;
        lock (_lock) { item = _queue.Dequeue(); }
        Handle(item);
    }
}
```

This replaces `Thread.Interrupt` with the cancellation token's `WaitHandle` participating in `WaitAny`.
